1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:05,147
Brush the soil from a dark, heavy blade. It is iron.

2
00:00:05,147 --> 00:00:10,015
A plough tipped with iron can break hard, wet soil that a wooden plough cannot.

3
00:00:10,015 --> 00:00:16,543
People who used this tool could clear forests and feed more people. What did iron change?

4
00:00:32,417 --> 00:00:39,903
How do we know? From both. Archaeologists dug up the grey painted pottery and iron tools of the Ganga plains.

5
00:00:39,903 --> 00:00:48,493
The later Vedic texts, the Samaveda, Yajurveda and Atharvaveda, and the Brahmanas, describe the same world.

6
00:01:02,883 --> 00:01:10,820
The Later Vedic age runs from about one thousand to six hundred BCE. The centre moved east, to the Ganga-Yamuna doab.

7
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:14,763
Iron came into use, from about one thousand BCE.

8
00:01:14,763 --> 00:01:23,145
Archaeologists link this age with Painted Grey Ware, a fine grey pottery with black designs. Farming became the main occupation.

9
00:01:23,145 --> 00:01:30,225
Wheat and rice were grown, and the iron-tipped plough was used. Larger harvests supported larger kingdoms.

10
00:01:30,225 --> 00:01:39,047
Tribes joined to form janapadas. The Kuru and Panchala kingdoms were the most powerful. The Kuru capital was Hastinapura.

11
00:01:39,047 --> 00:01:47,793
Kings now performed grand rituals, such as the Rajasuya, the Vajapeya and the Ashvamedha. The assemblies lost power.

12
00:01:47,793 --> 00:01:58,525
Society was divided into four varnas: Brahmana, Kshatriya, Vaishya and Shudra. Life was also ordered into stages, called ashramas.

13
00:01:58,525 --> 00:02:01,735
Women lost some of their earlier freedom.

14
00:02:16,117 --> 00:02:21,336
Scholars disagree. How did the varna order begin, and how rigid was it?

15
00:02:21,336 --> 00:02:26,550
One view holds that a hierarchy of varna grew from Vedic society and hardened over time.

16
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:37,727
Another view holds that caste took shape through a long historical process, in which occupations, kin groups and the state all played a part, and that the texts describe an ideal.

17
00:02:52,117 --> 00:02:59,620
Carve this tip into memory. Iron, Painted Grey Ware. Kuru, Panchala, four varnas.

18
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:06,999
Iron plough, grey pottery, two kingdoms and four varnas. That is the Later Vedic age in four steps.

19
00:03:21,383 --> 00:03:37,149
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Name this metal tool that opened the forests to farming. The answer: an iron ploughshare.

20
00:03:37,149 --> 00:03:49,912
Number two. Which pottery is linked with the Later Vedic age? The answer: Painted Grey Ware. Number three.

21
00:03:49,912 --> 00:04:01,782
Which Kuru capital stands beside the Ganga? The answer: Hastinapura. Now one line for Mains.

22
00:04:01,782 --> 00:04:06,492
What changes did the Later Vedic age bring to society and polity?

23
00:04:24,883 --> 00:04:32,750
The ploughshare goes into its case. It is plain, but it helped to change the map of power. Three facts to take away.

24
00:04:32,750 --> 00:04:43,495
Iron tool of the Ganga plains. Linked with Painted Grey Ware. Larger farms, larger kingdoms. Next gallery: Voices of the Veda.

